#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162907} :activity/id #uuid "3bc6ea9c-5bd9-5b09-adae-b354a31133fa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050038 :topic/id #uuid "27efd169-3481-580f-84b8-e14a6c1981dc" :topic/math-academy-id 2994} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163245 :tutorial/content "We often want to approximate a discrete random variable $X$ by a continuous random variable $Y$. To do this, we need to consider how to treat the values of $Y$ that lie between the discrete values of $X$.\n\nFirst, recall that if $X = 2$ *to the nearest integer*, then\n\n$$\n1.5\\leq X \\lt 2.5\n$$\n\nNow consider the following scenario:\n\n- $X\\in\\big\\{\\ldots, 0,1,2, 3\\ldots\\big\\}$ is a *discrete* random variable with *integer* support\n- $Y\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$ is a *continuous* random variable that we will use to approximate $X$\n\nTo approximate the probability\n\n$$\nP(X = 2)\n$$\n\nusing the random variable $Y$, we need to calculate\n\n$$\nP(1.5 \\leq Y \\lt 2.5)\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Y$ is continuous, this probability is equal to\n\n$$\nP(1.5 \\leq Y \\leq 2.5)\n$$\n\nWe can think of the required values of $Y$ as lying within the interval contained within a circle of radius $0.5$ centered at $Y=2$.\n\n![](images/8a/8af3b4a73cdf37169e0841ce7ef9bbe31fefd171cdeacc33dcc8cedca0facfe8.png)\n\nAdjusting the values of a discrete random variable so that it can be approximated using a continuous random variable is called a **continuity correction.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "b16688d9-1366-509d-a221-3eb89bdade89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5008 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5efe4efe-88c4-5775-9066-19d8f36ca425" :step/math-academy-id 31300 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162025 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161817 :question/id #uuid "0e332257-0d6f-59e6-a2cd-8cc53dd50173" :question/math-academy-id "e-8491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7be1caf7-728f-5c51-b3da-64f3f7ff6fa9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162693}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Probability Involving a Discrete Random Variable at a Single Point"} :step/id #uuid "52795ad0-0ab5-5c10-a15f-c110cc25db8a" :step/math-academy-id 31585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162026 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161818 :question/id #uuid "eb9d63bd-dfbb-5c37-bdd0-2dc0b9d5ac6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d41f010-e1b6-5da8-ab37-c584d84dd5b6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162679}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Probability Involving a Discrete Random Variable Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "76cbd9e2-0111-5bb0-9573-59d1fce2a507" :step/math-academy-id 31587 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162027 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161819 :question/id #uuid "a6430e40-7b8f-5d1b-a842-ec7cc6013abe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d691e3a-f9c5-5c9d-863c-533abdebb948" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162681}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Probability Involving a Discrete Random Variable Over an Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "629db14c-6e82-5798-a452-f00da2492dfb" :step/math-academy-id 31588}] :activity/title "Approximating Discrete Random Variables as Continuous" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162160}]}
